      "candidate_text": "We first derive the formula from the converging-lens construction of a real image in Figure 179, with the object beyond the object-side focus.\nBoth foci have the same letter in the figure; each triangle below uses the focus on its own side. This construction does not represent every signed imaging case.\nThe triangles $\\triangle ABF$ and $\\triangle DOF$ are similar, so:\n$\\frac{\\lvert OD\\rvert}{\\lvert AB\\rvert}=\\frac{\\lvert OF\\rvert}{\\lvert BF\\rvert}$\nSince $\\lvert OD\\rvert=\\lvert A_1B_1\\rvert$, we obtain:\n$\\frac{\\lvert A_1B_1\\rvert}{\\lvert AB\\rvert}=\\frac{\\lvert OF\\rvert}{\\lvert BF\\rvert}=\\frac{F}{d-F}$ (6.15)\nLikewise, $\\triangle COF$ and $\\triangle A_1B_1F$ are similar, giving:\n$\\frac{\\lvert A_1B_1\\rvert}{\\lvert OC\\rvert}=\\frac{\\lvert B_1F\\rvert}{\\lvert OF\\rvert}$\nUsing $\\lvert OC\\rvert=\\lvert AB\\rvert$, we get:\n$\\frac{\\lvert A_1B_1\\rvert}{\\lvert AB\\rvert}=\\frac{f-F}{F}$ (6.16)\nEquating the right-hand sides of (6.15) and (6.16):\n$\\frac{F}{d-F}=\\frac{f-F}{F}$\nHence:\n$F^2=fd-fF-Fd+F^2$\nOr $fF+Fd=fd$.\nThe denominators and distance product are nonzero in this construction. Dividing each term by $Ffd$ gives:\n$\\frac{1}{d}+\\frac{1}{f}=\\frac{1}{F}$ (6.17)\nIn terms of the optical power of the lens, this is:\n$\\frac{1}{d}+\\frac{1}{f}=D$ (6.18)",
